WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.182
The same table can support several aggregate measures in one query.

00:00:04.182 --> 00:00:10.157
This example summarizes sales using functions such as SUM, AVG, MIN, and MAX.

00:00:10.157 --> 00:00:12.227
Before running it, make a prediction.

00:00:12.227 --> 00:00:19.122
The total quantity and total revenue answer different questions, while the minimum and maximum show the range

00:00:19.122 --> 00:00:25.647
of individual values. Run the query in the browser and inspect each result column.

00:00:25.647 --> 00:00:31.652
Notice that PostgreSQL can calculate all of these measures from the same set of source rows.

00:00:31.652 --> 00:00:37.852
The important idea is not to add every aggregate function just because you can.

00:00:37.852 --> 00:00:40.982
Choose measures that support the analytical question.

00:00:40.982 --> 00:00:46.982
A decision-maker usually needs a small set of clearly named metrics rather than every possible summary.

00:00:46.982 --> 00:00:51.642
When reading the example, do not read punctuation one character at a time.

00:00:51.642 --> 00:00:59.127
Start with the purpose of the query, then identify the input rows, the operation being applied,

00:00:59.127 --> 00:01:01.117
and the shape of the expected output.

00:01:01.117 --> 00:01:08.957
In a real setting such as a retail report comparing revenue across products and regions,

00:01:08.957 --> 00:01:15.392
this habit makes code reviews much easier because you can explain what the query is supposed to guarantee

00:01:15.392 --> 00:01:16.957
before discussing syntax.

00:01:16.957 --> 00:01:23.479
After running it, inspect a few records manually and check that the result matches that explanation.
